# Accepted DAST (OWASP ZAP) findings — issue #368.
#
# Read by backend/cmd/zapgate. Format: one rule per line,
# `<rule> <risk> <url-regex>`, where:
#   - rule is a ZAP pluginid (e.g. 40045) or alertRef (e.g. 10055-6, a specific
#     sub-finding under one plugin) — an exact match — or `*`
#   - risk is `high`, `medium`, or `*` (case-insensitive)
#   - url-regex is a Go regexp matched against the finding's URL, or `*`
# A `#` comment on the rule line is captured as the justification and logged on
# every [ACCEPT]. This is the same "ignore-list with justification" shape as
# android/.mobsf and docker/cis-hardening.ignore: a High/Medium finding on the
# app is a hard failure unless a line here accepts it, and every acceptance
# must carry a reason that survives review.
#
# The canary (dastcanary, localhost:7301) is deliberately vulnerable and its
# findings are expected — those never need an entry here; zapgate treats them
# as the self-test signal, not app findings.

# Spring4Shell (40045) is a Java/Spring RCE via data binding; the backend is
# Go/Gin and the image contains no Java, no JVM and no Spring at all, so the
# vulnerable code path does not exist to be reached.
#
# The rule fires on a *response shape*, not on evidence of Spring: it POSTs
# `class.module.classLoader.DefaultAssertionStatus=nonsense` and treats a 400
# as a positive, because in a real Spring app a 400 means the binder processed
# the malicious property. Here the 400 is `middleware.ValidateJSONMiddleware`
# rejecting a malformed body — which every endpoint taking a validated JSON
# body does, to every malformed body. The reported evidence is literally
# `HTTP/1.1 400 Bad Request` and nothing else.
#
# Scoped to `.*` deliberately. This entry used to name a single endpoint
# (`check-password-strength`), which was the wrong axis: the justification is a
# property of the *image* (no Spring anywhere), not of one route, so URL
# scoping could only ever mean "accepted until the same false positive lands on
# a different endpoint". That is exactly what happened — the first run of this
# job to produce a report at all flagged /login/2fa,
# /password-reset/confirm and /password-reset/request instead, and
# check-password-strength not at all.
40045 high .*  # false positive: Go/Gin app, no Java/JVM/Spring in the image; 40045 reads a 400 from JSON validation as a hit

# The SPA CSP is a deliberate, documented posture (docker/nginx.conf:21 and
# frontend/nginx.conf). style-src 'unsafe-inline' is required by MUI's
# CSS-in-JS (inline <style> tags); the XSS-relevant directive, script-src,
# stays 'self' with no unsafe-inline. Only the nginx-served SPA HTML carries
# this CSP — the JSON API emits default-src 'none'
# (backend/middleware/security_headers.go), which does not trip this rule.
10055-6 medium .*  # MUI CSS-in-JS requires style-src 'unsafe-inline'; script-src stays 'self'

# form-action is left unset: the SPA makes no HTML <form> submissions (every
# write is a same-origin fetch/XHR), so an unrestricted form-action has no
# reachable sink. Candidate for `form-action 'none'` in a future CSP hardening
# pass — deliberately not part of this (DAST-infrastructure) ticket.
10055-13 medium .*  # SPA makes no HTML <form> submissions; form-action has no sink

# ZAP 40018's boolean-based SQL-injection check flagged the `email` parameter
# of POST /api/v1/login: it reports that the responses for `... AND 1=1 -- `
# and `... OR 1=1 -- ` "differed". This is a false positive.
#
# The login query is GORM-parameterised — controllers/user_controller.go builds
# it as db.Where("email = ?", identifier) / db.Where("username = ?", identifier),
# never string concatenation (the repo-wide "parameterised SQL only" posture;
# docs/security/asvs-l2.md). The entire payload lands as one bound literal and
# matches no row; there is no dynamic-SQL sink on this path.
#
# The heuristic misfires because the auth path's response genuinely varies with
# the input string for reasons that are not SQL: the per-identifier account
# rate-limiter (middleware.GetAccountRateLimiter) flips 401 -> 429 with a
# retry_after body partway through ZAP's back-to-back probes; enumeration-
# resistant handling returns an identical 401 for "no such user" and "bad
# password"; and bcrypt only runs once a row is found, so response timing/size
# shift with the probe value. ZAP's own evidence field is empty, confidence is
# Medium, and it notes the original probe value also returned no data.
#
# Scoped to /login, not `.*`: a 40018 hit on any other route is a real signal
# and must not be pre-accepted.
40018 high /api/v1/login$  # false positive: login query is GORM-parameterised (email = ? / username = ?); 40018's boolean heuristic trips on the auth path's rate-limit/enumeration/bcrypt-driven response variance, not SQL

# Same 40018 boolean-based SQL-injection check, now on the `device_token`
# parameter of POST /api/v1/auth/device/session (the device-grant exchange,
# issue #722): "... AND 1=1 -- " vs "... OR 1=1 -- " responses "differed".
# False positive, and a stronger case than /login's:
#
# controllers/device_grant_controller.go builds the lookup as
# db.Where("token_hash = ? AND revoked_at IS NULL",
# services.HashDeviceGrantToken(input.DeviceToken)) — GORM-parameterised, and
# the bound value is a SHA-256 hash of the submitted token
# (services/device_grant_service.go), not the raw string. Any payload,
# injection or not, hashes to an unrelated hex digest that matches no
# token_hash row; there is no dynamic-SQL sink and no way for the payload's
# content to reach the query shape at all.
#
# Same response-variance mechanism as /login causes the misfire: this route
# carries the same AuthRateLimitMiddleware (per-IP) ahead of the lookup, so
# ZAP's rapid back-to-back probes from one IP flip some responses 401 -> 429
# partway through; an unknown/invalid grant returns the same
# ErrInvalidCredentials() 401 as a revoked one (enumeration-resistant). ZAP's
# evidence field is empty here too and confidence is Medium, not High —
# same signature as the accepted /login entry above.
#
# Scoped to this one route, not `.*`: a 40018 hit on any other route is a
# real signal and must not be pre-accepted.
40018 high /api/v1/auth/device/session$  # false positive: query is GORM-parameterised AND the bound value is a SHA-256 hash of the input (services.HashDeviceGrantToken), not the raw token — no payload content can reach the query; same rate-limit-driven response variance as the accepted /login entry

# A third instance of the same 40018 boolean-based SQL-injection check, now on
# the `language` parameter of POST /api/v1/register: the responses for
# "en' AND '1'='1' -- " and "en' AND '1'='2' -- " "differed". False positive.
#
# Register has no dynamic SQL at all. Its only database work is
# db.Model(&models.User{}).Count(&userCount) — no predicates, no input — and
# db.Create(&user), which GORM compiles to a parameterised INSERT: every field,
# including Language, is a bound value, and no request input can reach query
# text (controllers/user_controller.go, the repo-wide "parameterised SQL only"
# posture; docs/security/asvs-l2.md).
#
# What the heuristic actually sees is response variance, from two sources, none
# of them SQL:
#
#   - Register carries AuthRateLimitMiddleware — the same process-wide
#     authLimiter as /login (middleware/rate_limiter.go), a hardcoded burst of
#     50 at one token per 500ms. The DAST compose env raises only the *API*
#     limiter (API_RATE_LIMIT_BURST/INTERVAL), not the auth one, so ZAP's rapid
#     back-to-back probes from one IP exhaust the auth bucket and some responses
#     flip to 429 partway through — the same mechanism already accepted for
#     /login and /auth/device/session.
#   - Register's own semantics are order-dependent: the first valid body returns
#     201 (and grants admin on an empty user table), every later one returns 409
#     ErrAlreadyExists, while an injected `language` fails the
#     oneof=en de it es fr validator with a 400. So the same parameter can
#     produce three different status/body pairs depending on sequence, which is
#     exactly the kind of "the data returned changed" signal 40018 looks for.
#
# ZAP's evidence field is empty, confidence is Medium (not High), and systemic
# is False — the same signature as the two accepted entries above.
#
# Scoped to this one route, not `.*`: a 40018 hit on any other route is a real
# signal and must not be pre-accepted.
40018 high /api/v1/register$  # false positive: register has no dynamic SQL (db.Model(...).Count with no predicates + a GORM-parameterised Create); 40018's boolean heuristic trips on the shared authLimiter flipping probes to 429 plus register's 201/409/400 ordering variance, not SQL

# A fourth instance of the same 40018 boolean-based SQL-injection check, now on
# the `password` parameter of POST /api/v1/check-password-strength: the
# responses for the "1=1" and "1=2" payload variants "differed". False positive,
# and the strongest case of the four.
#
# This endpoint has NO database access at all. controllers.CheckPasswordStrength
# (backend/controllers/user_controller.go:331) binds a single `password` field
# and calls middleware.EvaluatePasswordStrength
# (backend/middleware/password_strength.go), which computes entropy, a 0-4
# score and feedback as pure functions of the string — no GORM, no session, no
# credentials. The route is classified "stateless zxcvbn utility" in
# routes/session_minting_route_gate_test.go:102. Different payloads therefore
# produce genuinely different scores/feedback by design, which is exactly the
# response variance 40018's boolean heuristic misreads as SQL.
#
# ZAP's evidence field is empty, confidence is Medium (not High), and systemic
# is False — the same signature as the three accepted entries above.
#
# Scoped to this one route, not `.*`: a 40018 hit on any other route is a real
# signal and must not be pre-accepted.
40018 high /api/v1/check-password-strength$  # false positive: stateless zxcvbn utility with no database access (CheckPasswordStrength -> EvaluatePasswordStrength, a pure function of the password); 40018's boolean heuristic misreads the score/feedback variance as SQL

# Plugin 6 (Path Traversal) flagged the `password` parameter of
# POST /api/v1/register: uri http://localhost:7300/api/v1/register, attack
# `/register`, evidence empty, confidence Low. False positive.
#
# Register has no filesystem or path handling at all. The handler binds a JSON
# body (username, password, email, language), evaluates the password's strength,
# hashes it with bcrypt, and does one GORM-parameterised Create (plus a
# predicate-free Count for the first-user-is-admin rule). No request input
# reaches a path, a filename, a URL or a command; there is no sink a traversal
# payload could reach.
#
# Plugin 6's heuristic is response-variance based, the same mechanism already
# accepted for the four 40018 entries above: register carries
# AuthRateLimitMiddleware (the process-wide authLimiter; the DAST compose env
# raises only API_RATE_LIMIT_BURST/INTERVAL, not the auth bucket), so ZAP's
# rapid back-to-back probes from one IP flip some responses 400/201 -> 429
# partway through, and register's own 201 (first valid body) / 409 (already
# exists) / 400 (validator) ordering variance makes the "data returned changed"
# signal look meaningful. ZAP's evidence field is empty and confidence is Low —
# the same signature as the accepted entries above. It is also intermittent:
# nothing on this route changed between the last clean nightly and the run that
# reported it.
#
# Scoped to this one route, not `.*`: a plugin-6 hit on any other route is a
# real signal and must not be pre-accepted.
6 high /api/v1/register$  # false positive: register has no filesystem/path handling (bcrypt-hashed password + a GORM-parameterised Create); plugin 6's variance heuristic trips on the authLimiter flipping probes to 429 plus register's 201/409/400 ordering, not a traversal

